      <h1 id="stage-22-software-domains" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 22: Software domains<a href="#stage-22-software-domains" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Document the <code>mod-script-pipe</code> interface with elements from DITA's software and programming domains. Create a scripting reference for command syntax, parameters, messages, and platform-specific pipe locations.</p>
      <p>Create a task that references <code>samples/export-mp3.py</code> through <code>&lt;coderef&gt;</code>. Mark up a terminal session with <code>&lt;screen&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;userinput&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;systemoutput&gt;</code>.</p>
      <p><strong>Optional module.</strong> See <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path">Choose a learning path</a> for the starting checkpoint and the next core lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 35 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 21 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/run-the-gate">Check your work</a> to see the result for your own project.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-the-scripting-reference" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: The scripting reference<a href="#step-1-the-scripting-reference" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/scripting-reference.dita</code>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n<reference id=\"scripting-reference\">\n  <title>Scripting commands</title>\n  <shortdesc>The scripting commands most useful for batch work, their parameters and the responses they return.</shortdesc>\n  <prolog>\n    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n    <critdates>\n      <created date=\"2026-03-01\"/>\n    </critdates>\n    <metadata>\n      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"expert\"/>\n      <category>reference</category>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>scripting</keyword>\n        <keyword>mod-script-pipe</keyword>\n        <keyword>automation</keyword>\n        <indexterm>scripting<indexterm>commands</indexterm></indexterm>\n      </keywords>\n    </metadata>\n  </prolog>\n  <refbody>\n    <section>\n      <title>Syntax</title>\n      <p>A scripting command is a command name, a colon, and zero or more <varname>Name</varname>=<varname>Value</varname> parameters.\n      <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> replies with the command's output followed by a status line.</p>\n      <syntaxdiagram>\n        <title>Scripting command</title>\n        <groupseq>\n          <kwd>CommandName</kwd>\n          <delim>:</delim>\n          <groupseq importance=\"optional\">\n            <repsep/>\n            <var>Name</var>\n            <delim>=</delim>\n            <var>Value</var>\n          </groupseq>\n        </groupseq>\n      </syntaxdiagram>\n      <p>A synopsis in one line: <synph><kwd>Export2</kwd><delim>:</delim> <var>Filename</var>=<var>path</var> <var>NumChannels</var>=<var>1|2</var></synph>.</p>\n    </section>\n    <refsyn>\n      <title>Commands</title>\n      <parml>\n        <plentry>\n          <pt>\n            <cmdname>SelectAll:</cmdname>\n          </pt>\n          <pd>Selects every track from start to end. No parameters.</pd>\n        </plentry>\n        <plentry>\n          <pt><cmdname>Normalize:</cmdname> <parmname>PeakLevel</parmname>=<varname>dB</varname></pt>\n          <pd>Normalizes the selection so its peak sits at <varname>dB</varname>, typically <codeph>-1</codeph>.</pd>\n        </plentry>\n        <plentry>\n          <pt><cmdname>Export2:</cmdname> <parmname>Filename</parmname>=<varname>path</varname> <parmname>NumChannels</parmname>=<varname>n</varname></pt>\n          <pd>Exports the selection to <varname>path</varname>; the extension selects the format.\n          <option>NumChannels=1</option> forces mono.</pd>\n        </plentry>\n        <plentry>\n          <pt><cmdname>GetInfo:</cmdname> <parmname>Type</parmname>=<varname>Commands|Tracks|Clips</varname></pt>\n          <pd>Returns a JSON description of the requested objects.</pd>\n        </plentry>\n      </parml>\n    </refsyn>\n    <section>\n      <title>Responses</title>\n      <p>Every reply ends with a status line.\n      Success looks like <msgph>BatchCommand finished: OK</msgph>; a failure names the command:</p>\n      <msgblock>Export2: Filename=episode.mp3 NumChannels=1\nBatchCommand finished: Failed!</msgblock>\n      <p>The <apiname>send</apiname> function in the sample script (<filepath>export-mp3.py</filepath>, listed in <xref href=\"exporting-from-a-script.dita\"/>) returns the whole reply; check it for <msgnum>Failed!</msgnum> before sending the next command.</p>\n    </section>\n    <properties>\n      <prophead>\n        <proptypehd>Platform</proptypehd>\n        <propvaluehd>Pipe location</propvaluehd>\n        <propdeschd>Notes</propdeschd>\n      </prophead>\n      <property platform=\"linux mac\">\n        <proptype>Linux, macOS</proptype>\n        <propvalue>\n          <filepath>/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.to.<varname>uid</varname></filepath>\n        </propvalue>\n        <propdesc>A named pipe per user id; open it for writing.</propdesc>\n      </property>\n      <property platform=\"windows\">\n        <proptype>Windows</proptype>\n        <propvalue>\n          <filepath>\\\\.\\pipe\\ToSrvPipe</filepath>\n        </propvalue>\n        <propdesc>A Windows named pipe; open it in text mode.</propdesc>\n      </property>\n    </properties>\n  </refbody>\n</reference>"} lang="xml" title="topics/scripting-reference.dita" />
      <p>The <em>Responses</em> section names the sample script with <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code> and links to the task that lists it, <code>exporting-from-a-script.dita</code>. The link goes to the task and not to the <code>.py</code> file, because the build does not copy <code>samples/</code> into the output: the script reaches the reader as the listing that <code>&lt;coderef&gt;</code> pulls into the task.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the syntax diagram</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;syntaxdiagram&gt;</code> is a block that describes syntax as a tree of groups. <code>&lt;groupseq&gt;</code> is a sequence; its children are <code>&lt;kwd&gt;</code> for a literal keyword, <code>&lt;delim&gt;</code> for a delimiter, <code>&lt;var&gt;</code> for something the reader supplies. The outer sequence is <em>CommandName</em> <code>:</code> then an optional inner sequence, <code>importance="optional"</code>, of <em>Name</em> <code>=</code> <em>Value</em>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;repsep&gt;</code> in the inner sequence is the separator between repetitions of that group. It is empty here because the separator is a space, and the project's formatter trims whitespace-only text. It comes <em>first</em> inside the <code>&lt;groupseq&gt;</code> it applies to, before the keywords and variables: the content model of a group is a title, then an optional <code>&lt;repsep&gt;</code>, then the items. The demo at the end puts it in the wrong place.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;synph&gt;</code> is the inline form: a syntax phrase with the same <code>&lt;kwd&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;delim&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;var&gt;</code> children, for a one-line synopsis in running text.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the parameter list</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;refsyn&gt;</code> is the reference section for syntax; a <code>&lt;reference&gt;</code> body may hold it beside plain <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>s.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;parml&gt;</code> is a definition list for parameters: each <code>&lt;plentry&gt;</code> has a <code>&lt;pt&gt;</code> (the term) and a <code>&lt;pd&gt;</code> (its description). Inside them, <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code> is the command, <code>&lt;parmname&gt;</code> a named parameter, <code>&lt;varname&gt;</code> a value the reader supplies, <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> a switch that selects a behavior, <code>&lt;codeph&gt;</code> a literal value.</li>
      <li>Everything a reader types or reads on a screen gets the element that says what it is, and a stylesheet can render <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code> in bold and <code>&lt;varname&gt;</code> in italic without you deciding it here.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the messages</strong> <code>&lt;msgph&gt;</code> is a message quoted inline; <code>&lt;msgblock&gt;</code> is a message as a block, whitespace preserved like <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code>; <code>&lt;msgnum&gt;</code> is the message's identifier, here the status word <code>Failed!</code>. <code>&lt;apiname&gt;</code> is the name of a function, class or method in an API.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the properties table</strong> <code>&lt;properties&gt;</code> is a reference table with three fixed columns: <code>&lt;proptype&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;propvalue&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;propdesc&gt;</code>, with an optional <code>&lt;prophead&gt;</code> naming them. Each <code>&lt;property&gt;</code> row carries a <code>platform</code> attribute, so a filtered build keeps only the rows for its platform. No Windows-filtered deliverable includes the scripting reference: the <code>podcaster-linux</code> build drops the Windows row, and the unfiltered builds show both rows. <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code> with a <code>&lt;varname&gt;</code> inside it names the pipe with the user id as a variable.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-the-task-and-the-sample" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: The task and the sample<a href="#step-2-the-task-and-the-sample" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Add </strong><code>samples/export-mp3.py</code> The task in the next step pulls this Python script into its listing. In the <strong>Explorer</strong>, right-click <code>my-audacity-guide</code>, choose <strong>New Folder</strong>, and enter <code>samples</code>. Then add the script to the new folder in one of these ways:
      <ul>
      <li>Add the file from the checkpoint <code>tutorial/22-software-domains</code> of the <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/set-up#the-sample-project">sample project</a>. Download the file from its <a href="https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial/blob/tutorial/22-software-domains/samples/export-mp3.py">GitHub page</a>, or save it from a copy of the sample project on that branch. Then right-click <code>samples</code> in the <strong>Explorer</strong> and choose <strong>Add Files...</strong>. In the file chooser, use <strong>Look In</strong> to go to the folder that holds <code>export-mp3.py</code>, select the file, and click <strong>Add</strong>. The editor copies the file into <code>samples</code>. You can also drag the file from your desktop onto <code>samples</code> in the <strong>Explorer</strong>.</li>
      <li>Right-click <code>samples</code>, choose <strong>New File</strong>, and enter <code>export-mp3.py</code>. The editor creates the file empty. Type or paste the listing, and save the file.</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Export the open Audacity project as MP3 through mod-script-pipe.\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nif sys.platform == \"win32\":\n    TO_PIPE = \"\\\\\\\\.\\\\pipe\\\\ToSrvPipe\"\n    FROM_PIPE = \"\\\\\\\\.\\\\pipe\\\\FromSrvPipe\"\n    EOL = \"\\r\\n\\0\"\nelse:\n    TO_PIPE = f\"/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.to.{os.getuid()}\"\n    FROM_PIPE = f\"/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.from.{os.getuid()}\"\n    EOL = \"\\n\"\n\n\ndef send(to_pipe, from_pipe, command: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Send one command and return the reply, which ends with an empty line.\"\"\"\n    to_pipe.write(command + EOL)\n    to_pipe.flush()\n    reply = \"\"\n    while True:\n        line = from_pipe.readline()\n        if line == \"\" or (line == \"\\n\" and reply):\n            return reply\n        reply += line\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    target = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else \"episode.mp3\"\n    with open(TO_PIPE, \"w\") as to_pipe, open(FROM_PIPE) as from_pipe:\n        for command in (\"SelectAll:\", \"Normalize: PeakLevel=-1\",\n                        f\"Export2: Filename={target} NumChannels=1\"):\n            print(send(to_pipe, from_pipe, command).strip())"} lang="text" title="samples/export-mp3.py" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/exporting-from-a-script.dita</code>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n<task id=\"exporting-from-a-script\">\n  <title>Exporting from a script</title>\n  <shortdesc>Enable mod-script-pipe, then normalize and export the open project from a Python script.</shortdesc>\n  <prolog>\n    <author type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team</author>\n    <critdates>\n      <created date=\"2026-03-01\"/>\n    </critdates>\n    <metadata>\n      <audience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"expert\"/>\n      <category>automation</category>\n      <keywords>\n        <keyword>scripting</keyword>\n        <keyword>Python</keyword>\n        <keyword>export</keyword>\n        <indexterm>scripting<indexterm>from Python</indexterm></indexterm>\n      </keywords>\n    </metadata>\n  </prolog>\n  <taskbody>\n    <prereq>\n      <p>Python 3 is installed, and a project is open in <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>.</p>\n    </prereq>\n    <steps>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Choose <menucascade><uicontrol>Edit</uicontrol><uicontrol>Preferences</uicontrol><uicontrol>Modules</uicontrol></menucascade>, set <uicontrol>mod-script-pipe</uicontrol> to <uicontrol>Enabled</uicontrol>, and restart <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>.</cmd>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Save the sample script as <filepath>export-mp3.py</filepath>.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <codeblock outputclass=\"language-python\"><coderef href=\"../samples/export-mp3.py\"/></codeblock>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Run it, naming the output file.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <screen><userinput>python3 export-mp3.py episode.mp3</userinput>\n<systemoutput>BatchCommand finished: OK\nBatchCommand finished: OK\nBatchCommand finished: OK</systemoutput></screen>\n        </info>\n        <steptroubleshooting>\n          <p>If the script stops with <msgph>FileNotFoundError</msgph>, the pipe does not exist: <keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> is not running or the module is not enabled.\n          On Linux, <cmdname>ls</cmdname> <filepath>/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.*</filepath> shows whether the pipes are there.</p>\n        </steptroubleshooting>\n      </step>\n    </steps>\n    <result>\n      <p>The project is normalized to -1 dB and exported as mono MP3.\n      Change <parmname>NumChannels</parmname> to <codeph>2</codeph> for stereo.</p>\n    </result>\n  </taskbody>\n  <related-links>\n    <link href=\"scripting-reference.dita\"/>\n  </related-links>\n</task>"} lang="xml" title="topics/exporting-from-a-script.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the coderef</strong> <code>&lt;codeblock outputclass="language-python"&gt;&lt;coderef href="../samples/export-mp3.py"/&gt;&lt;/codeblock&gt;</code> is the whole listing. <code>&lt;coderef&gt;</code> is an empty element that, at build time, is replaced by the text of the file it points at, relative to the topic. The script lives once, in <code>samples/</code>, where it can be run and tested; the topic never goes stale against it. <code>outputclass</code> on the <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code> becomes a class on the <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> in the HTML5 output, <code>language-python</code>, the convention that syntax highlighters such as highlight.js and Prism read. In the check's build of the full guide, <code>out/full/topics/exporting-from-a-script.html</code>, the listing is in place:
      <CodeBlock code={"<pre class=\"pre codeblock language-python\"><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Export the open Audacity project as MP3 through mod-script-pipe.\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nif sys.platform == \"win32\":\n    TO_PIPE = \"\\\\\\\\.\\\\pipe\\\\ToSrvPipe\"\n    FROM_PIPE = \"\\\\\\\\.\\\\pipe\\\\FromSrvPipe\"\n    EOL = \"\\r\\n\\0\"\nelse:\n    TO_PIPE = f\"/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.to.{os.getuid()}\"\n    FROM_PIPE = f\"/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.from.{os.getuid()}\"\n    EOL = \"\\n\"\n\n\ndef send(to_pipe, from_pipe, command: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Send one command and return the reply, which ends with an empty line.\"\"\"\n    to_pipe.write(command + EOL)\n    to_pipe.flush()\n    reply = \"\"\n    while True:\n        line = from_pipe.readline()\n        if line == \"\" or (line == \"\\n\" and reply):\n            return reply\n        reply += line\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    target = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) &gt; 1 else \"episode.mp3\"\n    with open(TO_PIPE, \"w\") as to_pipe, open(FROM_PIPE) as from_pipe:\n        for command in (\"SelectAll:\", \"Normalize: PeakLevel=-1\",\n                        f\"Export2: Filename={target} NumChannels=1\"):\n            print(send(to_pipe, from_pipe, command).strip())</code></pre>"} lang="plaintext" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the script</strong> The script follows the pipe example that Audacity publishes for <code>mod-script-pipe</code>:
      <ul>
      <li>Audacity creates two pipes, one for commands and one for replies. On Linux and macOS they are <code>/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.to.&lt;uid&gt;</code> and <code>/tmp/audacity_script_pipe.from.&lt;uid&gt;</code>, where <code>&lt;uid&gt;</code> is your user id, which <code>os.getuid()</code> returns. On Windows they are the named pipes <code>\\.\pipe\ToSrvPipe</code> and <code>\\.\pipe\FromSrvPipe</code>, written with doubled backslashes in a Python string. Each command on Windows ends with <code>\r\n\0</code>, and on the other platforms with <code>\n</code>.</li>
      <li>The script opens both pipes once, in one <code>with</code> statement, and sends the three commands through them.</li>
      <li>Each reply is the command's output, the status line, and then an empty line. <code>send()</code> reads the reply line by line and returns when it reaches that empty line. A single <code>read()</code> would wait for the end of the file, and that never comes while Audacity holds the pipe open, so the script would hang after the first command.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the screen</strong> <code>&lt;screen&gt;</code>, from the user interface domain, is a block for what a terminal shows, whitespace preserved. Inside it, <code>&lt;userinput&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;systemoutput&gt;</code>, from the software domain, mark what the reader typed and what came back, so a stylesheet can tell them apart. The <code>&lt;steptroubleshooting&gt;</code> from stage 06 covers the common way the step fails: if the pipe does not exist, opening it raises <code>FileNotFoundError</code>, marked with <code>&lt;msgph&gt;</code>. <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code> mark the command that checks for the pipes.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the maps</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -61,6 +61,10 @@\n     <topicref href=\"topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita\" keys=\"podcast-workflow\"/>\n   </topichead>\n \n+  <topichead navtitle=\"Automation\">\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/exporting-from-a-script.dita\"/>\n+  </topichead>\n+\n   <topichead navtitle=\"Reference\" id=\"reference\">\n     <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" keys=\"formats\" locktitle=\"yes\">\n       <topicmeta>\n@@ -69,6 +73,7 @@\n     </topicref>\n     <topicref href=\"topics/effects-reference.dita\" keys=\"effects\"/>\n     <topicref href=\"topics/effect-presets.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/scripting-reference.dita\" keys=\"scripting\"/>\n   </topichead>\n   <topichead navtitle=\"Glossary\">\n     <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\"/>"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
